chroma v2.27.0 with lexers and styles built when they are asked for.

upstream parses the config of all 279 embedded lexers and all 74 embedded
styles when the package is initialised, keeps them for the life of the
program, and every client pays for it — chroma is reached through glamour's
ansi package, which our markdown rendering always imports. measured on a
resting client: 511 kB of live heap (365 kB lexers, 146 kB styles).

here the embedded lexers are reduced to an index of what they can be found by
(name, aliases, file patterns, mime types, whether they analyse text), and a
lexer's config, rules and analysers are built the first time a lookup, a file
name match or a snippet reaches it. styles are parsed on the first Get of
their name. Registry is filled as styles are built, so a caller that registers
its own theme (our codeTheme) and glamour's lookup by name both still work.

two smaller changes come with it. a LexerRegistry can now carry a Lookup
function, so a rule that names another lexer (chroma's Using mutator) reaches
one that is not built yet; and the ignored file suffixes moved to an exported
IgnoredSuffixes, since the lexers package matches file names itself now.
styles/compat.go is gone: its package-level vars snapshotted every embedded
style at init, which is the cost this patch exists to remove, and nothing we
link uses them.

output is unchanged: cli/internal/tui/chroma_patch_test.go renders every
language a reply names and every file name a diff names, in both of our
palettes, against testdata/chroma_highlights.golden, which was taken from the
stock module (build with a -modfile whose go.mod drops the replace).

to bump chroma: copy the new release over third_party/chroma, delete its
tests, CI and testdata, and apply this patch
(patch -p1 -d third_party/chroma < third_party/chroma.patch).

